1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Alexandra Davidovna (1808–1848), was born in St. Petersburg and was the godchild of Emperor Alexander I. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1826, she dissolved her betrothal with Alexander Mordvinov (1798-1858), the son of an admiral, which caused strong condemnation in the world. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Later, in Berlin, she was engaged to Prince Vasily Repnin (1806–1880), the son of N. G. Repnin-Volkonsky, but at the request of the bride, the wedding was again canceled. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1832, in Rome, she met with Count Albert de la Ferrone, the son of the former French ambassador to Russia. In 1834 she married him, contrary to the advice of his mother, who considered this marriage extremely unsuccessful in al
2 CONC l respects. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She died on February 9, 1848 in Paris, after 14 happy years of marriage. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The sister of Count de la Ferrone, writer Paulina Craven, in 1868 published the novel The Sister's Story, in which she described the love story of her brother with Alexandra Alopeus.
