1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Historieböcker Pieksämäki landsförsamling - Pieksämäki country parish Vigda: Vigd 30.3.1749 Man Sulkava Cr.Befall .m. Joh. Wilh. Meinander Hustru Prost dr. J. Maria Krogia
2 CONT National Archives: Sulkava parish - Main and Deed books 1756-1764 (I Aa:1), section 87: Sulkava Södra byn: Wilhelm Meinander/Maria Krogia, Lang, Krook, Holm / Cited 19.7.2020
2 CONT National Archives: Sulkava parish - Pää- ja Ripkirikjat 1756-1764 (I Aa:1), section 87: Sulkava Södra byn: Wilhelm Meinander/Maria Krogia, Lang, Krook, Holm > Same as above, but easier to zoom with smart devices
2 CONT National Archives: Sulkava Parish > Main and Ripkirkjat 1781-1785 (I Aa:3), section: 97, Pardala
2 CONT National biography: Meinander, Johan Wilhelm (1715 - 1784) Crown Prince, sawmill owner, county secretary Johan Wilhelm Meinander served for decades as crown prince in the fiefdom under Savo, near the border with Russia. He perform
2 CONC ed his duties carefully, but he has gone down in history mainly because of his saw projects.
2 CONT Johan Wilhelm Meinander (September 23, 1715 Sweden – February 26, 1784 Sulkava) was a sawmill owner and a pioneer in the direct foreign trade of timber, but also a farmer, crown prince and county secretary.[1]
2 CONT Meinander's parents were Crown Prince Olof Meinander and Anna Magdalena Wargentin. His first wife was Birgitta Poppia (c. 1716-1748) and his second from 1749 was Maria Krogia (1731-1797). Meinander succeeded his father as the crow
2 CONC n prince of the fiefdom under Savo in 1741, but was also the owner of the Iitlahti farm and a farmer in Sulkava. The Miettula single-frame sawmill he built in 1765 received an annual quota of 1,500 logs and permission to trade saw
2 CONC n timber directly to Russia in 1768, partly with the support of Anders Henrik Ramsay. The second sawmill founded by Meinander in Sulkava Kuhakoski was mainly managed by his son Paul. The burghers of Loviisa opposed Meinander's bus
2 CONC iness, but the local peasants, on the other hand, saw new earning opportunities for themselves in the wood trade. Meinander also had the support of the nobles of the Savo region. In 1772, an official decision was made that all saw
2 CONC mills in Savo and Pohjois-Häme are allowed to export lumber to Russia past their home town of Loviisa. The decision was in principle against Sweden's economic policy at the time, but it was also an indication of the economic liber
2 CONC alization that began at the end of the 18th century. Like their father, Meinander's children and their families developed into businessmen and civil servants, among other things. His son Adolf Meinander, the governor of Oulu Count
2 CONC y, was ennobled in the early 19th century as Edelsvärdi.[1]
2 CONT SULKAVA, TANNILA Sulkava Tannila village No. 1 Rustholli Area 329 ha (1831) Torppia 6 (1831) The name of the house comes from Tannis, who were the owners in the 16th and 17th centuries. The estate ownership started when the crown 
2 CONC prince J.W. Meinander took rustholli into his cultivation.
2 CONT KIISKILÄ, ERNESTILÄ KARTANO Juva Kiiskilä village No. 1 Rustholli Area 1,225 ha (1830) Torppia 13 (1830) The Kiiskilä name comes from Vilppu Kiiskinen, whose family owned it until 1750. At the turn of the 18th and 19th centuries, 
2 CONC the manor began to be called Ernesti after its owner at the time, Ernst Fabritius. In 1750, Johan Wilhelm Meinander became the owner.
2 CONT Wikipedia: Johan Wilhelm Meinander
2 CONT
